#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707} :activity/id #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/id #uuid "c60f74c0-bf82-529d-b9dd-b8bfe8e2cc4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121048 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+6t \\\\ y &=-2+5t \\\\ z &=5-t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n\\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-3}{6} &=t \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{5} &=t \\\\ \\frac{z-5}{-1} &=t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3245 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" :step/math-academy-id 17885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119637 :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413273}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"} :step/id #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" :step/math-academy-id 17886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119638 :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413270}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"} :step/id #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" :step/math-academy-id 17887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119639 :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447025}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" :step/math-academy-id 17888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121055 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3316 :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" :step/math-academy-id 18457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119656 :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495153}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" :step/math-academy-id 18458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120015}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447027} :activity/id #uuid "4c26b5f1-ca5b-5728-8463-5b32e35af136" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/id #uuid "c60f74c0-bf82-529d-b9dd-b8bfe8e2cc4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "628ba019-e287-5e6c-9c20-0bd08a8d96f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-4},\\,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260725} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e6a649-3a81-512d-94bb-f14eb562c95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261472} {:answer/id #uuid "c7117f4f-8b9b-5207-8fcb-870aceb867f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261635} {:answer/id #uuid "e335a903-50df-50af-bbcb-1934b2874ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261871} {:answer/id #uuid "f563cde3-4746-533c-bf25-d120ecc2da8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{-4},\\,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261472} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c9a7cd2-cbdc-54f5-8320-283acf5adf7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6f2a4e2-bb20-56c7-957d-47666291e84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127527" :question/problem "![](images/87/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6c98daf6-3ae9-572b-ac90-c2ddf8f54388"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0fb049-79eb-55e2-a0ca-4fdfc5e2665f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336144} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeb2205-47f3-5952-a1db-f53aaf324af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-2}{-9},\\,z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337572} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5d36b6-450f-5f52-a838-c89ca6718db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=z-11,\\,y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337732} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b304a4-4128-55d7-9b97-804366ca23bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-3}=\\frac{y-9}{2},\\,z=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337806} {:answer/id #uuid "d10667a1-fec6-59c1-a83d-dc77b0c42b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=z-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336144} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f571153-e1e5-51d4-9174-5e999fd92b23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f24fb14-8cf2-586c-8b58-d1952f0a20ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-71039" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-9)}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=11:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7-3t \\\\ -9+2t \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ part and instead write $z=11$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "819efd4e-ea7b-530c-8492-698aae4bfae3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbe3ab9-a7ef-5513-96b0-4bb81eecfdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-3}{5}=z-2,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447012} {:answer/id #uuid "58eab78c-4412-5b45-b6ad-1d2c084a66a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+4=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447015} {:answer/id #uuid "7287e364-e5ae-5892-9f4f-1be7926ae48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447017} {:answer/id #uuid "77cf8edb-f11a-593d-b22b-70517358473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z+1}{2},\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447018} {:answer/id #uuid "bd35653f-734b-565e-9879-e2cd580e5dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447020} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa198ef-bba1-5d57-9850-a75e16ce7498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47833d2d-e4b4-5480-a72b-ba5cccc746dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71043" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-4)}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+4}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+4}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-4:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5+3t \\\\ 1+2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+4}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,x=-4\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "842ff690-e68c-5ec7-a29c-4509d3d2ffad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34899314-00b5-5497-adbb-b71a1ac21237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447013} {:answer/id #uuid "3aff6a90-29cb-5fbb-a280-7d4c79fc7881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{4}=\\frac{z-7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447014} {:answer/id #uuid "6083d104-e8d5-54a9-91d4-825774ce35f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z+7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447016} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f8e739-90fc-54a0-8ff2-caa9d9c9da58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-6}=\\frac{y+3}{4}=\\frac{z-7}{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447019} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c04908-246a-5ae0-92f2-f471b0d7a518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447021} :answer-field/id #uuid "58349fdc-d11c-5b54-9386-0a54b8d936ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61ab7a4c-ae26-516b-9967-3f8072e6be8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127468" :question/problem "![](images/01/01943e80947c833ef37241fb836db96afd61731b9873cd061277a7453b34f3d8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,7)$ and $Q(3,-1,1).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨-1,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aaebba90-5fd2-5222-b798-afec0cfd305e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9e78327-b43d-5969-8d3b-bed4609b49e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367228} {:answer/id #uuid "415707ec-16c3-52eb-ae48-60f27d55babf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367229} {:answer/id #uuid "ace39f4a-2512-5b36-8884-7fa037704cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+5}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{-3}=\\frac{z+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367230} {:answer/id #uuid "e8680259-b972-51c1-a413-a39306c9e5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{-5}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z+7}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367231} {:answer/id #uuid "e0439e86-3ea3-5b81-8a8b-d0a949ba059f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+5}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{-2}=\\frac{z+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367228} :answer-field/id #uuid "4010e133-bb74-590a-aaad-0744ebb64de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "841b2c54-bf92-5c9a-835e-03e5b492bd88" :question/math-academy-id "q-127408" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-5,2,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e724c68f-5680-5be4-aa66-0e3b05d39d11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447029}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447011}]}
